      "id": 1,
      "text": "Harrison Church discovered coal near the Hams Fork River in 1868, leading to the founding of Diamondville.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11,
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diamondville%2C_Wyoming"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Diamondville was named for the superior-grade local coal that resembled black diamonds.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        5,
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diamondville%2C_Wyoming"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The town was built specifically to house miners, many of whom lived in a settlement known as shack town.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16,
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diamondville%2C_Wyoming"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "An Oregon Short Line Railroad station was established in Diamondville in 1894.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diamondville%2C_Wyoming"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "By the early 1900s, Diamondville featured a theater, a photography studio, and thirteen different saloons.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diamondville%2C_Wyoming"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The Daly Hotel in Diamondville was a three story building with fifty rooms, a dining room, and a barbershop.",
